    {
      "id": "_TDeZLwRsFzGHE5WJHRwM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 316,
      "y": 1114,
      "width": 601.0000000000001,
      "height": 1625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aK",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 196010258,
      "version": 2000,
      "versionNonce": 798037330,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790146526704,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "3. HashMap internally uses an array of references to Node\nobjects. Each Node represents a key-value entry. Node is an\nimpelementation class of Entry interface .\n\n4. This array is called a hash table because hashing is used\nto determine the bucket/index where an entry is stored.\n\n5. The default initial capacity of HashMap is 16. However,\nthe internal array is lazily created when the first entry is\ninserted.\n\nstudent.put( \" Amit \" , 101 ) ;\nstudent.put( \" Sumit\" , 102 ) ;\nstudent.put( \" Karan\" , 103 ) ;\n\n\n6. Here java will find the index for Amit \n\nh=key.hashcode\n\nhash = h ^ (h >>> 16)\n\nindex = (n - 1) & hash lets say --> 14\n\nso the Entry will be stored in index 14 .\n\n7. Let say for student.put( \" Sumit\" , 102 ) ;\n\nindex = 3\n\n8. for student.put( \" Karan\" , 103 ) ;\n\nindex = 14 again then the second node will be linked to first\nnode .\n\n9. In this way storing object is fast in hash table .\n\n10. Searching is also in similar way for ex -\n student.get( \" Amit \" );\n\nh=key.hashcode\n\nhash = h ^ (h >>> 16)\n\nindex = (n - 1) & hash\n\n11. Load factor of HashMap is 0.75 by default. For capacity\n16, the resize threshold is 16 × 0.75 = 12. When the\nthreshold is reached, HashMap resizes the table (normally 16\n→ 32). The load factor applies to the total number of\nentries, not to the number of nodes at an individual index.\n\nIn this way chances of collision becomes less and storing\nsearching keep efficient .\n\n12. When HashMap resizes, it creates a new, larger internal\narray (usually double the previous capacity) and transfers\nthe existing Nodes/Entries into the new array. Their\nindexes may change because the table size has changed.\n\n13. If a single bucket contains 8 or more Nodes, HashMap\nmay convert the bucket's linked list into a Red-Black Tree.\nHowever, this happens only when the table capacity is at\nleast 64. If the capacity is less than 64, HashMap resizes\nthe table instead.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "3. HashMap internally uses an array of references to Node objects. Each Node represents a key-value entry. Node is an impelementation class of Entry interface .\n\n4. This array is called a hash table because hashing is used to determine the bucket/index where an entry is stored.\n\n5. The default initial capacity of HashMap is 16. However, the internal array is lazily created when the first entry is inserted.\n\nstudent.put( \" Amit \" , 101 ) ;\nstudent.put( \" Sumit\" , 102 ) ;\nstudent.put( \" Karan\" , 103 ) ;\n\n\n6. Here java will find the index for Amit \n\nh=key.hashcode\n\nhash = h ^ (h >>> 16)\n\nindex = (n - 1) & hash lets say --> 14\n\nso the Entry will be stored in index 14 .\n\n7. Let say for student.put( \" Sumit\" , 102 ) ;\n\nindex = 3\n\n8. for student.put( \" Karan\" , 103 ) ;\n\nindex = 14 again then the second node will be linked to first node .\n\n9. In this way storing object is fast in hash table .\n\n10. Searching is also in similar way for ex -\n student.get( \" Amit \" );\n\nh=key.hashcode\n\nhash = h ^ (h >>> 16)\n\nindex = (n - 1) & hash\n\n11. Load factor of HashMap is 0.75 by default. For capacity 16, the resize threshold is 16 × 0.75 = 12. When the threshold is reached, HashMap resizes the table (normally 16 → 32). The load factor applies to the total number of entries, not to the number of nodes at an individual index.\n\nIn this way chances of collision becomes less and storing searching keep efficient .\n\n12. When HashMap resizes, it creates a new, larger internal array (usually double the previous capacity) and transfers the existing Nodes/Entries into the new array. Their indexes may change because the table size has changed.\n\n13. If a single bucket contains 8 or more Nodes, HashMap may convert the bucket's linked list into a Red-Black Tree. However, this happens only when the table capacity is at least 64. If the capacity is less than 64, HashMap resizes the table instead.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
